<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-27a" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - 4-vectors</title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider two four-velocities
        <m>
            \underset{\sim}{V_1},\underset{\sim}{V_2}
        </m>.
        Their scalar product is
        <md>
            \underset{\sim}{V_1}\cdot\underset{\sim}{V_2}=\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}(1-\vec{v}_{1}\bullet\vec{v}_{2})
        </md>.
        Physically, what does this represent?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <title>Asymmetric decay</title>
    <p>
        A pion (mass <m>M_{\pi}</m> ) decays into a muon (mass <m>M_{\mu}</m> ) and
        a neutrino (approximate as massless): <m>\pi^{+}\to \mu^{+} +\nu</m>.
        What are the energies of the muon and the neutrino?
    </p>

    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw a before and after picture and a four-vector diagram that
                    represents (in the lab frame) the conservation of momentum
                    <m>\underset{\sim}{p_\pi}=\underset{\sim}{p_\mu}+\underset{\sim}{p_\nu}</m>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    We know that <m>p_{\mu}^{2}=m_{\mu}^{2}</m>, so square the
                    equation for <m>\underset{\sim}{p_\mu}</m> above.
                    Hint: since we are in the lab frame, the dot product of
                    <m>\underset{\sim}{p_\pi}</m> with another vector should be quite
                    simple.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use your expression to solve for <m>E_{\nu}</m>, the energy of
                    the neutrino.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Now use the conservation laws (or really: your vector diagram
                    from the first part!) to solve for the energy of the muon, the
                    momentum of the muon (in magnitude), and the speed of the muon.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    The pion has a mass of 139 MeV, and the muon has a mass of 103
                    MeV (MeV is a unit of energy, what does this mean?).
                    Find the energy of the muon (in MeV!) and its speed ( <m>v/c</m>
                    ).
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    If you find the muon to be at rest after the decay, how fast was
                    the pion moving?
                    What neutrino energy would you observe (good luck with that!)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
